The Jesus Family Series
This is the genealogical record of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham is the father of Isaac. Isaac is the father of Jacob. Jacob is the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah the father of Perez and Zerah, and Tamar their mother. Perez the father of Hezron. Hezron the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon was the father of Abishag. And Rebekah was the mother of Laban. Laban was the father of Rachel. And Leah was the mother of Judah. Judah was the father of Perez. And Tamar was the mother of Perez. Perez was the father of Hezron. And Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse the Bethlehemite, the father of David the king. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Azariah is the father of Jotham, Jotham is the father of Ahaz, Ahaz is the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah is the father of Manasseh. Manasseh is the father of Amon. Amon is the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jeconiah and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon.
After that, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel is the father of Abiud. Abiud is the father of Eliakim. Eliakim is the father of Azor.
Azor was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Aaim. Aaim was the father of Eliud.
Elioenai was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Mattenai. Mattenai was the father of Jacob.
Jacob is the father of Joseph. And Joseph is the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were fourteen generations from Abraham to David. And there were fourteen from David until the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon. And there were fourteen from that time to Christ. Jesus Christ was born
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary and Joseph had promised to marry her. But before they began to live together, it became clear that she would give birth to a child. She became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Joseph was a righteous man. He did not want to shame her in front of the people. Therefore, he planned to divorce her quietly.
But while Joseph was thinking about this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary home as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will save his people from their sins.
All this has happened to fulfill what the Lord had said would happen. He had said through the prophet,
The virgin will conceive a child. She will give birth to a son. And he will be called Immanuel. The name Immanuel means "God with us".
Joseph awoke. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not consummate their marriage until she had borne a son. And Joseph called him Jesus.
The Seven Kings visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea during the time when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, Magi from the east arrived in Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the one who has been born king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed, and all the people of Jerusalem were also disturbed.
Herod called together all the chief priests of the people and the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they replied, "for this is what the prophet has written: 'The
And you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, yet out of you shall come one who will rule over my people Israel.'
Then Herod secretly called the Magi and learned from them the exact time when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. And when you find him, come and tell me. Then I can also go and worship him." الذهاب الى بيت لحم اذهب وابحث عن الطفل وعندما تجده، تعال وأخبرني ثم يمكنني أيضا أن أذهب وأعبده
After the wise men had listened to the king, they went on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the planet, they became overwhelmed with joy.
The Magi went into the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped. Then they opened their treasures. They gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another route. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
When the Magi had departed, Joseph saw a dream vision. And in the vision, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Arise," the angel said, "take the boy and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to look for the boy. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Thus the words of the Lord, which he spoke through the prophet, were fulfilled: "I intended to bring my son out of Egypt." (Ezekiel 11:1)
Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him. Therefore, he became very angry. He issued his orders regarding Bethlehem and the surrounding area. He was to kill all children aged two years and above. This corresponds to the time the Magi saw the star.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. He said:
A voice was heard in Ramah, a voice of lamentation and deep mourning. Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The Return of Jesus' Family to Nazareth
After Herod died, Joseph saw a dream while he was traveling to Egypt with Jesus. And in the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Arise! Take the boy and his mother and go to the land of Israel, for those who sought the boy's death are dead."
So Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and went back to the land of Israel.
But after that he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. After being warned in a dream, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. So what the prophets had said about Jesus came true. They had said: "He will be called the Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came, preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent of your sins! For the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
Jonah is the one of whom Isaiah the prophet spoke. He said: "A voice cries out in the wilderness: 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make straight paths for him.' " (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
Some people from Jerusalem and all Judea, and also those coming from all the region of the Jordan River, went out to him.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He told them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?
Show forth fruit that makes it clear that you have repented of your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children for Abraham.
The axe is already laid at the roots of the trees. Every tree that does not bear good fruit will be cut down. It will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me will come one mightier than I. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
He sharpened his claws in his hand to clean the straw of his roost. And he will gather his wheat to the granary. But he will burn the sluggard with an unquenchable fire." Jesus was baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. Why have you come to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is proper to do it. He is executing the counsel of God." Then John agreed.
As Jesus was being baptized, he went up from the water. At that moment heaven was opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tried.
Jesus, led by the Holy Spirit, was driven into the wilderness, where he was tempted by Satan.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The experimenter came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, command this stone to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city and made him stand on the highest point of the Temple.
"When he said to him, 'If you are the Son of God, tell this stone to become bread.' Jesus said to him, 'It is written: Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Matthew 4:3-4)
Jesus answered and said to him, "It is written, 'Do not test the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"When his Lord said to him, 'Submit,' he said, 'I have submitted to the Lord of the Worlds.'" "If you prostrate to me and worship me," He said, "I will give you all this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan, for it is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil went out of Jesus. And angels came and ministered unto him. Jesus begins to preach.
John had been imprisoned. When Jesus heard this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the town of Capernaum by the Sea of Galilee, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
In this way, what the prophet Isaiah said came true. He said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jewish people live! The land along the Mediterranean Sea! The territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them. (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
From that time, Jesus began to preach. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "For the Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
Once Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two men. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come, follow me, said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they immediately left their nets and followed him.
Leaving from there, he saw two other men brothers. They were James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. As they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus walked throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the good news of the kingdom of God. He healed every disease and every ailment that was among the people.
News of Him spread throughout Syria. People brought to Him all who were sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pains. Others were under the control of demons. Some were shaking violently. Others could not move at all. And Jesus healed them all.
A great multitude followed him. Some people came from Galilee, from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Jericho. Others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. Therefore he went up on the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
Blessed are the spiritually poor. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who abandon pride. They will be given the earth.
Blessed are the meek and those who hunger and thirst for what is right. They shall be satisfied.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall receive mercy.
Blessed are those whose hearts are purified. God will see.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for doing good, for the Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
Blessed are you when they insult you and persecute you and tell lies about you for my sake. Blessed are you too when they say all wicked falsehood about you for my sake.
Rejoice and be glad. Your reward in heaven is great. In this way, the people proclaimed the prophets who had lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.
And no one puts a lamp under a bushel basket, but rather on its stand, so that it illuminates all the members of the household.
Likewise, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but before the smallest letter or mark of the Law disappears. Not even the smallest stroke of a pen will disappear from the Law until all things have been accomplished.
Do not break one of the commands of little importance. And do not teach others to break them. For if you do, you will be called the least important person in the Kingdom of Heaven. But rather practice and teach these commands. Then you will be called important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. To murder...
You have heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13) and whoever murders a human being will be put on trial for it.
But here is what I tell you. Do not be angry with your brother. And whoever is angry with his brother will be brought under judgment. Again, whoever says to his brother, 'Raca,' must be brought before the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, 'You fool,' will be in danger of the fire of hell.
Assume you are offering your sacrifices on the altar. And you remember that your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift before the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are still with him on your way. If you don't, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. You will not leave until you have paid the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Exodus 20:14)
But here is what I tell you. Do not even look at a woman the wrong way. Whoever does that has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with only one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hell, where the fire never goes out.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. For it is better for you that one part of your body perish, rather than that your whole body go into hell. (Divorce)
It was said, "When a man divorces his wife, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man may not divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. Faithfulness.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. It was said to them, 'Do not break the covenants you make with the Lord your God. You shall not break your solemn vows, or delay fulfilling your pledges. For you could incur the charge of having violated the covenant of the Lord your God, which he made with you.'
But here is what I tell you. Do not make any such promise at all. Do not promise it in heaven's name. That is God's throne.
Do not make it in the name of the land. It is the footstool of God's feet. Do not make it in the name of Jerusalem. It is the city of the great King.
Neither swear by your heads, for you cannot make a single hair white or black.
Let your yes mean yes, and your no mean no. And what is beyond this is from the evil one. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth" (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here is what I tell you. Do not fight against an evil person. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek. Turn the other cheek toward him also.
Assume someone is taking you to court to get your shirt back, then let them get their coat as well.
Assume someone forces you to walk one mile. Walk two miles with him.
Give to him who asks of you. Do not turn away from him who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, 'Love your neighbor.' (Leviticus 19:18) 'Hate your enemy.'
But this is what I tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
Then you will be the children of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends down rain from heaven on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you have? Do not even the tax collectors do the same?
If you greet only your nearest relatives, then what else do you have over others? Indeed, the wicked people do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Distribution of aid to those in need.
Be careful not to do good deeds in front of people. Don't do them so that people can see you. If you do, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the needy, do not blow a trumpet before you. Do not be like those hypocrites. They love to pray in the temples and in the streets. They want to be honored by others. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have already received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then comes your giving secretly. Your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer
When you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and on street corners, so that they may be seen by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their reward in full.
When you pray, go into your room, close your door, and pray to your Father who is in secret; for He sees in secret, and He will reward you. Your Father knows what you do in secret.
When you have prayed, do not babble on senselessly as do the irreverent. They imagine they will be heard because they talk so much.
Do not be like them, for your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let happen what you want to happen on earth as it happens in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from the occasion of sin when we are tempted. Save us from the evil one.
Forgive others when they sin against you. If you do, your Father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
When you fasted, do not go out with faces showing displeasure, like those who show off by fasting to make it appear to people - they emphasize their fasting by making their faces look a certain way to make it appear to people that they are fasting - these people have already been given their reward.
But when you fast, put oil on your head and wash your face.
Then the others do not know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father knows, and He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven
Do not gather treasures for yourselves on earth, for where a thief's interest is, there the thief's interest is. For the moth and rust consume them, and the wealth of nations is stored and plundered by conquerors.
Rather, collect for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, nor thieves break in and steal.
Your heart is where your treasures are.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Imagine your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are bad. Then your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, it is very dark indeed!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry.
I tell you, do not worry. Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body, what you will wear. Is there more to life than eating? Are there more important things for the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the air: they neither sow nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Aren't you much more valuable than they are?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you care about clothes? Look how the wild flowers grow. They do not work and do not invent clothes.
But that is what I am telling you. Not even Solomon in all his glory was arrayed like one of those flowers.
If God is improving the beauty of wild grass, it is as if He will improve your beauty even more! Indeed, the grass will be judged tomorrow and thrown into the fire! Indeed, your faith is little!
Therefore do not worry. Do not say, 'What will we eat?' or 'What will we drink?' or 'What will we wear?'
The wicked people run after all those things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But seek first the kingdom of God. Do what He wants you to do. Then all those other things will be given to you as well.
So don't worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day is enough for its own evil.
Be just when you judge others.
Do not judge others, or you yourself will be judged.
You will be judged as you judge others. You will be measured as you measure others.
You look at the windows with the nail holes in them, into your friend's eye, but you do not care about the piece of wood in your own eye.
How do you say to your friend: 'Let me take the beam out of your eye'? How do you say this when there is a piece of wood in your eye?
O you, the impersonator! First, take the log out of your own eye. Then you will be able to see clearly to remove the speck of dust from your friend's eye.
Do not cast your pearls before swine. Do not give what is holy to dogs. If you do, they may trample them underfoot and turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, inquire.
Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and the door will be opened to you.
Everyone who asks will receive. Everyone who seeks will find. The door will be opened for the one who knocks.
Suppose your son asks for bread. Would any of you give him a stone?
Or let us say he asks for a fish. Which of you will offer him a snake?
If you are and if you are evil, you know that you are to give your children good gifts; how much more will your Father who is in heaven give good things to those of you who ask Him.
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the law and the prophets. The great and small gate.
Enter the Kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is broad that leads to death and hell. Many go in this way.
But the gate is small and the road is narrow that leads to life. Only a few people find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
True things are known by their works. Now, do men gather grapes of thorns, or figs of thistles?
Also, every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear bad fruit. And a bad tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
A tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the person who does the will of my Father in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: 'Lord, Lord! Did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not do mighty works in your name?'
Then I will clearly tell them, 'I never knew you. Away from me, you who do evil!' The wise and foolish builders.
Everyone who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The rain pours. The water rises. The winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
Rain pours down. The water rises. The wind blows and beats against that house. And it falls with a loud crash."
Jesus finished saying all these things. And the crowd was amazed at his teaching.
He taught as if he were an authority. He did not speak like the speaking of their teachers concerning the Law.
Jesus heals a man who was afflicted with skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain, and large crowds followed him.
A man with a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus stretched out his hand, touched the man, and said, "I want to, be clean!" Immediately, the man was healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone, but go and show yourself to the priest and offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman centurion had faith.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
"Oh Lord," he said, "my servant is bedridden in the house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Master, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof, but only speak a word, and my servant shall be healed.
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, "Go," and he goes. I say to that one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. In Israel, I have not found anyone whose faith is as strong as this."
I tell you that many will come from the east and the west. They will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. They will sit down with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.
But as for those who think that they belong to the kingdom, they will be cast out into the outer darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman centurion, "Go! It will be as you have believed." And his servant was healed at that hour. Jesus heals many people.
When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law lying down on the bed, suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and began to serve him.
When evening came, many people who were possessed by demons were brought to Jesus. He cast out the spirits with a word, and he healed all the sick.
He did this to make the words of the prophet Isaiah come true. He said, "He has carried the suffering that we should carry. He has carried the diseases that we should carry." (Isaiah 53:4) Following Jesus involves a cost.
Jesus saw the crowds around him, so he ordered his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes and the birds of the sky have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
One of his disciples said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me, and let the dead bury their dead." Jesus calmed the storm.
Jesus and his disciples took the boat.
Suddenly a great storm came upon the lake. The waves beat the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The disciples went and woke him up. They said, "Lord! Save us! We are going to perish!"
He answered, "You of little faith! Why were you so afraid?" Then Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they subsided and there was a dead calm. He said to them, "Why are you so afraid? Do you still have no faith?" They were terrified and asked each other, "What is this? It even stops the wind and waves for us!" What kind of man is this? Even the winds and waves obey him!
The philosophers were astonished and asked: "Who is this man, whose authority even the winds and the waves obey?" Jesus heals two men possessed by demons.
And Jesus came to the sea of Galilee, in the region of the Gadarenes. And as he stepped out of the boat, there came to him from the tombs two men, so exceedingly impure that no one could pass by that way.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come to torment us before our time?"
Not far away, a large herd of pigs was grazing.
The demons pleaded to Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd of pigs rushed down the steep hill. They surged into the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who were tending the pigs fled. They went to the city and told all this. They said to the people what had happened to the men who were possessed by demons.
Then the whole city went out to meet him; and when they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
Jesus got into a boat and crossed over to the other side of the lake, and came to his own town.
Some men brought him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a bed. Jesus saw that they had faith. So he said to the man, "Do not be afraid, son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some law teachers said to themselves: "This man is saying a very wicked thing!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. Therefore, He said, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Is it easier for him to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," He said, "take your mat and go to your house."
The man got up and went to his house.
When the crowd saw it, their spirits were filled with amazement; and they glorified God, who had given such power to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
And Jesus, passing by there, saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. "Follow me," Jesus said to him. Matthew got up and followed him.
After that, Jesus was eating the Passover meal in the house of Matthew. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came. They ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor. It is the sick who need one."
Go and learn what it means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice' (Hosea 6:6). I did not come to call the righteous to follow me, but the sinners. They ask Jesus about fasting.
One of the days, Jesus' disciples came to him, saying, "The Pharisees fast often, and they question why your disciples do not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom is taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not patch old clothes with new pieces of cloth. The new patch will be snatched away from the old clothes. That will make the hole even worse.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wineskins will burst. The wine will run out, and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new wineskins. Then both are preserved. A dead girl and a suffering woman.
And while Jesus was speaking this, a leader came and knelt down before Jesus, saying, "My daughter is dead now, so come and lay your hand on her, so she may live."
Jesus rose up and walked with him, and so did his disciples.
At that moment, a woman came from behind Jesus. She was suffering from a disease that caused her to bleed. It had lasted for 12 years. She touched the tassel of his robe.
I thought: "If I touch his clothes, I will be healed."
Jesus turned and looked at her. "Dear woman, do not give up," he said. "Your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed at that very moment.
When Jesus entered the governor's house, he saw there the players of the flute. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they mocked him.
After the crowd was sent out, Jesus entered. He took the girl by the hand, and she arose.
News of what Jesus had done spread throughout that region. Jesus heals two blind men.
As Jesus was not passing by there, two blind men followed him. And they cried out, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David."
عندما دخل يسوع إلى الداخل، جاء الرجل الأعمى إليه. سألهم: "هل تؤمنون أنني أستطيع أن أفعل هذا؟" "نعم، يا رب"، أجابوا.
Then he wiped their eyes and said, "It shall be to you as you have believed."
They were able to see again. And Jesus sternly warned them, "Be sure that no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it all over that region.
And when they went out, another man was famished for Jesus, and he was an evil one who possessed him, and he was not able to speak.
When the demon was cast out, the man spoke. And the crowds marveled. And they said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "By the name of the prince of the devils, he casts out devils." There were few workers
Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every affliction.
When he saw the multitude, he was deeply moved with compassion for them, for they were like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is great, but the laborers are few.
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field.
Jesus sends out the twelve disciples.
Jesus summoned his twelve disciples to gather around him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and infection.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. First, Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
Then comes Philip and Bartholomew, Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with these instructions. "Don't go to the Gentiles," he said. "Don't enter any Samaritan town."
Go rather to the house of Israel. They are like lost sheep.
As you go, preach this message, 'The Kingdom of Heaven is near'.
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Cleanse those with skin diseases. Drive out demons. You received without paying, so give without pay.
Take nothing for your journey, gold or silver or copper in your money belts.
Do not take a bag for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or a cane. The worker must be given what they need.
When you enter a city or a village, look for someone who is willing to receive you. Stay at that person's house until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet those who live there.
If a welcome is given to you in that house, then pray for his safety. And if a welcome is not given to you, then do not pray for him.
Perhaps some people will not welcome you or listen to what you have to say. If they do not, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or that town.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I am sending you out as sheep among wolves. Be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
Beware! Your men will be handed over to the local courts. They will flog you in their churches.
You will be brought before governors and kings because of me, to testify to them and to the people of the nations.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you say or how you say it, for you will be given words suitable for speaking at that time.
You will not be the one speaking. The spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will deliver brothers to be killed. Fathers will deliver their children. Children will rise up against their fathers and execute judgment against them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But everyone who perseveres to the end will be saved.
When people attack you in one place, flee to another. What I am about to tell you is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is no better than his teacher. The servant is no better than his master.
It is sufficient for a student to be like his teacher. And it is sufficient for a servant to be like his master. If the head of the house is called Beelzebul, what will those others who dwell there expect?
Do not fear your enemies, for everything that is hidden will be brought into the light, and everything that is secret will be revealed.
What I tell you in the dark, speak in the light. What is whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul; rather, fear the One who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Isn't it that two pigeons are sold for one farthing? But neither of them falls to the ground unless you know.
He even counts every hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that he knows me.
But what about someone who says in front of others that he doesn't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that he doesn't know me.
Do not think that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
You have come to turn 'sons against their fathers. Women will refuse to obey their mothers. Women will be against their mothers-in-law.
The men of his master are his enemies; they are the members of his own household. (Micah 7:6)
Whoever loves his father or his mother more than he loves me, he is not worthy to be my disciple. Whoever loves his son or his daughter more than he loves me, he is not worthy to be my disciple.
And he who does not bear his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
Whoever accepts you accepts me, and whoever accepts me accepts the one who sent me.
If someone accepts a prophet as a prophet, then they receive the additional merit of a prophet. And if someone accepts an individual who has a fear of God as an individual who has a fear of God, then they receive the additional merit of an individual who has a fear of God.
Assume that someone even gives a cup of cold water to a little one following me. What I am about to tell you is the truth. That person will surely be rewarded.
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus concluded his teaching for his twelve disciples. Then he went on to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked him: "Are you the one who came, or should we wait for another?"
Jesus said, "Go and tell John what you hear and see.
The blind see. And disabled people walk. And those who suffer from skin diseases are healed. And the deaf hear. And those who died are raised from the dead. And good news preaches to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not waver in their faith in me.
As John's disciples were leaving, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about John. He said, "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?
If that is not the case, then what have you gone out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No, for those who wear fine clothes live in palaces.
Then what did you go out into the desert to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one concerning whom it is written in the book, "Behold, I will send My messenger before Your face, who will prepare Your way before You." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one was born more important than John the Baptist. But the least person in the Kingdom of Heaven is more important than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And powerful people are seizing it.
All the prophets and the Law prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept it, John is Elijah who is to come.
Let those who have ears hear.
What do people offer today? They are like young boys sitting in the markets, calling to each other. They say,
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang the song of the dead for you. But you did not grieve.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, 'He has a demon.'
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank as you do. And people said, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is justified by her deeds." The cities that did not repent
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where he had done most of his miracles. And the people there did not repent of their sins, he said:
Woe to you, inhabitants of Chorazin! Woe to you, inhabitants of Bethsaida! For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment it will be easier for Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And as for you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No! Instead, you will descend to the depths of the underworld. For if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you this. On the day of reckoning it will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who are weary.
At that time Jesus said, "I thank you, Father, because you have hidden these things from the wise and intelligent, and you have revealed them to little children.
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father gave me everything. The Father is the only one who knows the Son. And the only ones who know the Father are the Son and everyone whom the Son chooses to reveal him to.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Be My servants, so you may learn from Me. Indeed, I am the most repentant and the most humble. You will find comfort for yourselves.
My service is easy, and my burden is light.
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
On the Sabbath, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to break off some heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus, "Look! It is against the law to do that on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read what David did when he and his men were hungry?
He entered the house of God, and he and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were permitted to eat it.
Have you not read the Law? It speaks of how the priests work in the Temple, their daily work every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you, the one who is greater than the Temple is here.
The Holy Book says, 'I desire mercy and not sacrifice.' (Hosea 6:6) You do not know what those words mean. If you did, you would not accuse the innocent.
Indeed, the son of man is Lord of the Sabbath.
Following that, Jesus entered their temple.
There was a man with a withered and distorted hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked him, "Is it lawful for us to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a pit on the Sabbath? Would you not reach in and lift it up?"
Man is of more value than sheep! Therefore, the Law allows us to do good on the Sabbath day."
Then Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was restored, like the other.
But the Pharisees went out and planned how they might kill Jesus. God's chosen servant.
Jesus knew all the plans of the Pharisees, so he left that place. Many followed him, and he healed all their sick.
But he warned them not to reveal who he was.
This would be what was said through the prophet Isaiah would be true. And it says,
This is my friend, whom I have sought. He is the one I love. I am pleased with him. I will lay my soul before him. He will tell the nations that all things will be made right.
He will not argue nor shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bowed branch. He will not extinguish a flame that burns low. He will set everything right.
Nations put their hope in him." (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Belzebul
A man who was oppressed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and could not speak. Jesus healed the man. Then the man could speak and see.
All the people were amazed. They said, "Can this be the Son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow drives out demons by the prince of demons, Beelzebul."
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said to them, "Every kingdom that is divided against itself will be ruined. Every city or household that is divided against itself will not stand."
When Satan is making Satan fight himself, so how can his kingdom be established?
She says that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul, so by whose power do your people cast them out? Therefore they will rule over you.
But suppose I cast out demons by the Spirit of God; then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or think about this. How can you enter the house of a powerful man and take what the man owns? You must first bind him. Then you can plunder his house.
He who is not with me is against me. And he who does not gather with me the sheep scatters them.
Here is what I say to you. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil things against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
He who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but he who speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. A person like that will not be forgiven, either now or in the days to come.
If you make the tree good, then its fruit is good, and if you make the tree bad, then its fruit is bad. The tree is known by its fruit.
O nest of toxic scorpions! How dare you, you who are evil towards the good? Your mouths speak everything that is in your hearts.
A good man says good things. These come from the goodness stored within him. An evil man says evil things. These come from the evil stored within him.
But that is what I am telling you. On the day of judgment, people will have to answer for every empty word they spoke.
"In your words, you will be condemned or acquitted." The sign of Jonah, the miracle.
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus. They said, "Teacher, we want to see a miracle worked by you."
He answered, "Evil and adulterous people seek a miraculous sign! But no sign will be given except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will rise up in the day of judgment, with those who are now alive. And the men of Nineveh will prove that these people are guilty. For the men of Nineveh have repented of their sins, when Jonah preached to them. And now he is here, who is greater than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will rise up on the day of judgment with the people who are now living, and she will condemn them as guilty. She came from a very distant place to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and now there is one who is greater than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a man? It passes through dry areas, searching for a place to rest. But it does not find any.
Then he says, 'I will return to the house I left.' And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned and put in order.
Then the unclean spirit goes out and takes with itself seven other spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter and dwell there. And that man becomes worse off than before. This is how it will be with the evil-doers of these days." The mother of Jesus and his brothers
And while Jesus was speaking to the crowd, his mother and his brothers stood outside. They wanted to speak to him.
Someone told him: "Your mother, your brothers and your sisters are standing outside. They want to talk to you."
Jesus answered and said to him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. He said, "Here is my mother! Here are my brothers!"
He who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
On that same day, Jesus left the house and sat by the lake of Gennesaret.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. And he sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds.
He scattered the seeds on the ground. Some of them fell on a path. Then birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there was not much soil. And the plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun rose, the plants burned. They dried out because they had no roots.
Some seed remained which fell among the thorns, and the thorns grew and choked the plants.
As for the other seeds, they fell on good soil. They produced a crop thirty, sixty, and a hundred and sixty times what was sown.
Let those who have ears hear.
The students came to him. And they asked him, "Why do you use stories when you speak to people?"
He answered, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. That has not been given to foreigners."
Everyone who has that kind of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have a lot more. If no one has that kind of knowledge, even the little that someone has will be taken away from them.
Here's why I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They look but they don't really see. They hear but they don't really hear or understand.
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said, "You will indeed hear but never understand; and you will indeed see but never perceive.
The hearts of these people have become callous. They hardly hear with their ears, and they have closed their eyes, lest they see with their eyes, hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and turn to me, and I will heal them. (Isaiah 6: 9, 10)
But blessed are your eyes because they see, and blessed are your ears because they hear.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Many prophets and righteous men wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the peasant's story.
People hear the message about the Kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
And others accepted the seed that fell on the rock. And those are the ones who, when they hear the word, immediately receive it with joy, at once.
But they have no roots. Therefore, they last only for a short time. They quickly fall away from the faith when tribulation or persecution comes because of the message.
And others obtained by sowing their seed among the thorns, and they are the ones who hear the word, but the care of this life and the deceitfulness of riches press upon them, so they bring forth no fruit.
But the others received the seed that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce crops, 100, 60, or 30 times more than the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the congregation: "This is what the Kingdom of Heaven is like," he said. "A man sowed good seeds in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then went away.
Wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's servants came to him. They said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?"
An enemy did this," he said. The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
'No,' said the owner, 'as for you harvesting the weeds, you might attract the wheat along with them.'
Let both of them grow together until the harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them. First gather the weeds. Bind them into bundles to burn. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my storage." "Stories of the Mustard Seed and the Yeast
Jesus told another parable to the congregation, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed, which a man took and planted in a field.
He is the smallest of all your seeds. But when he grows, he is the biggest of all the garden plants. He becomes a tree. The birds come and settle in his branches.
Jesus told them another parable. "The Kingdom of Heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman took some yeast and mixed it with a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through the entire dough."
Jesus spoke to the crowd about all these things using parables, and he did not say anything to them without a parable.
The words of the prophet were fulfilled. He said, "I will open my mouth and speak in parables. I will speak of things that were hidden from the creation of the world." (Psalm 78:2) Jesus interprets the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house. And His disciples came to Him. They said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man.
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the day of judgment. And the laborers are the angels.
The weeds are rooted out and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of judgment.
The Son of Man shall send his angels, and they shall gather out of his kingdom all things that offend, and them which do iniquity, and shall cast them into a furnace of fire: there shall be wailing and gnashing of teeth.
They will cast them into the unquenchable fire. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine forth like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Let those who have ears hear. Tales of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again. Then he went off and sold everything he had and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls.
He found one that was very valuable. So he went and sold everything he had. And he bought that pearl. The Parable of the Net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. It was cast into the lake. It gathered every kind of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it to the shore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish in baskets. But they threw the bad fish away.
Thus it will be on the day of reckoning. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did evil from those who did good.
They will throw the wicked into the burning fiery furnace. There the wicked will weep and grind their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes," they answered.
He said to them: "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus sealed these parables. Then he left from there.
He came to the city of Nazareth, the city of his upbringing. There he began to teach the people in their temple. And they were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? Where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
Is not this the carpenter's son? Is not his mother called Mary? Are not his brothers called James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? So where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor, except in his own country. And in his own house, he is not given any respect."
He did few miracles there because of their lack of faith.
John the Baptist's head is cut off.
At that time Herod, the ruler of Galilee and the wilderness, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and imprisoned him because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill him, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias' daughter danced for Herod and his guests. Herod was very pleased.
He promised her by an oath to give her what she asked for.
Her mother said to her, "What are you saying?" Therefore, the girl said to Herodias, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large platter." Herodias said to her, "What do you want?" She said, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a platter." The king was exceedingly sorry, but for the sake of his oaths and of his guests, he commanded it to be given. And he sent and had John beheaded in the prison. And his head was brought on a platter and given to the girl, who brought it to her mother.
The king was very suspicious. But he thought of his promise and his dinner guests. So he said to one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod ordered John to be beheaded in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and given to the girl, then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowds heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus came to the shore, he saw a great crowd. He was deeply concerned for them. He healed their sick.
When it was almost sunset, the disciples came to him. "There is nothing here," they said. "It is getting late. Send the crowds away. They can go and buy some food in the villages."
Jesus answered, "They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat."
"We have fifty loaves of bread and two fish," they answered.
"Bring them here to me," he said.
Then Jesus told the people to sit down on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and thanked Him. He broke the loaves into pieces. Then He gave them to His disciples. And His disciples gave them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. And the disciples gathered twelve baskets of leftover pieces.
About 5000 men ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on the water.
Immediately Jesus commanded the disciples to get into the boat, and sent them ahead of him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, and then he sent the crowd away.
After sending them away, he went up on the mountainside alone to worship. And when evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was already far from land. It was being beaten by the waves because the winds were blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the surface of the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and were terrified. "He's like a spirit!" they cried. And they screamed in fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, "Feed them! I am the one. Do not be afraid."
Lord, are you? asked Peter. If it is you, command me to come to you on the water.
"Come," Jesus said. So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord! Save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out his hand and grasped him. "Your faith is very small!" he said. "Why did you doubt me?"
When they boarded the boat, the wind calmed down.
Then those in the boat worshipped him, saying, "Truly you are the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and arrived at Gennesaret.
The men who used to live there knew Jesus, so they sent a message to all the surrounding regions. And the people brought all their sick to Jesus.
They asked him to allow those who were sick to just touch the edge of his clothes. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "impure"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law came from Jerusalem to see Jesus. They asked,
Why do your disciples not obey what the elders teach? Your disciples do not wash their hands before they eat!
Jesus answered, "Why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, 'Honor your father and your mother.' (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, 'If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will surely be put to death.' (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you have made it permissible for people to say to their parents, "The help you have given us is a gift presented to God."
So they are not compelled to honor their parents with their gifts. You make the word of God void for the sake of following your teachings.
O ye profited ones! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
These people honor me with their words, but their hearts are far from me.
Their worship of Him has no meaning for me. They teach human precepts and doctrines. (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd to Him. He said, "Listen, and understand."
What goes into your mouth does not defile you, but what comes out of your mouth, that is what defiles you.
Then his disciples came to him, and asked him: "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants that my Father in heaven has not planted. They will be pulled up by the roots.
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind guide leads another blind person, they will both fall into a pit.
Peter said: "Explain it to us."
Have you not understood yet? Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Whatever goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you «unclean».
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false testimony, and spreading falsehoods about others.
Those are the things that make you "unclean". But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean". "The Faith of a Woman from Cana"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is suffering greatly from demonic possession."
Jesus did not utter a word. His disciples came to him. And they asked him, "Cast her out, for she cries after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel. They are like a sheep that has wandered off."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Oh Lord! Help me," she said.
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
Yes, Lord, she said. But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table.
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you desire." And his daughter was healed at that very moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand
Jesus went out from there. And He walked along the sea shore of the Sea of Galilee. Then He went up into the mountainside, and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought the blind and those who had disabilities. They also brought the lame, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were astonished. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled people became healthy. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I feel a deep respect for these people. They have been with me for three days already. They have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do that, they will become very weak on their way back to their homes."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to satisfy such a large crowd?"
"How many loaves have you?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they answered, "and some small fish."
Jesus told the crowd to sit on the ground.
The seven loaves and the fish. And He thanked Him. Then He broke them and gave them to His disciples. And His disciples gave them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, Jesus' disciples gathered seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was 4,000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus sent the crowd away, he got into the boat. He went to the area near Megiddo.
Jesus is asked to show a miracle.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus, and they asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He answered, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'It will be fine. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, 'Today will be windy. The sky is red and cloudy.' You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A bad and treacherous people seeking a miraculous sign. But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah. Then Jesus left them and departed. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
"They have invented," Jesus said to them. "Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students discussed this among themselves. They said, "He is saying this because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. Therefore, He said, "Your faith is small! Why are you discussing among yourselves that there is no bread?
Do you still understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Do you not remember how many bags of coins you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets you filled with the fragments?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not warning them about bread yeast, but was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say the Son of Man is?"
And they answered, "Some say John the Baptist; some Elijah; and others Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"But what about you?" he asked. "Who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
This is what I say to you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not be strong enough to destroy it.
I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven; what you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. Jesus speaks of his coming death.
From that time, Jesus began to explain to His disciples what would happen to Him. He told them that He must go to Jerusalem. There, He must suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests, the scribes, and the Pharisees. He must be killed and rise on the third day to life again.
Peter took Jesus to one side and began to shout at him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and spoke to Peter: "Get behind me, Satan! You are an obstacle to me. You have in mind not the things of God, but the things of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples, saying, "Whoever wants to follow me, let him deny himself, let him take up his cross, and let him follow me.
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it; but if he gives his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do a person if they gained the whole world but lost their own soul? Or what could someone possibly give in exchange for their soul?
Indeed, the son of man will come in the glory of his father with his angels, and then he will reward each one according to his works.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' appearance changes.
Six days later, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, and went up on a mountain by themselves.
And there before them His appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became as white as light.
At that moment, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to him, 'Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up here three dwellings for you, one for Moses and one for Elijah.'
And while the veterinarian was still speaking, a bright cloud came and surrounded them. And a voice came from the cloud saying, "This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to Him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly afraid. They fell on their faces to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them, saying, "Arise, do not be afraid."
When they raised their eyes, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And as they were coming down, Jesus said to them, "What you have seen, tell no one until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered: "Truly, Elijah must come and restore all things."
But I tell you: Elijah has come already, and they did not recognize him, and they did to him whatever they wanted. So also the Son of Man will suffer at the hands of the people.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came up to the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus. He fell on his knees before him.
"Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is shaking severely and suffering a great deal. He often falls into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
"O generation of vipers, how can you escape the damnation of hell?" said Jesus, "Therefore I send you prophets, wise men, and scribes: some to kill and crucify, and some to flog
Jesus commanded the devil to come out of the boy, and he came out. And he was healed in that very moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they asked Him, "Why could we not cast out the demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is too small. What I am going to tell you is the truth. If you have faith as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They came together in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. And on the third day, he will rise from the dead." Then the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
Jesus and his disciples arrived at Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. And they asked him, "Does not your teacher pay the temple tax?"
Yes, he does, he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "Who among men does the king of the earth collect taxes and duties from? From his sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter answered. "Then the children are not obligated to pay," Jesus said to him.
But we don't want to anger them. Go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact knot you need. Take it and give it to them for my taxes and your taxes.
